  {
   "slug": "destinations",
   "title": "Destinations",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Every story is filed under the kind of landscape it happened in. Here is where we have been so far, and where we are heading next.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:columns {\"align\":\"wide\",\"className\":\"wl-tiles\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide wl-tiles\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:cover {\"url\":\"{{media:mountains}}\",\"dimRatio\":50,\"minHeight\":340,\"minHeightUnit\":\"px\",\"align\":\"none\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-cover alignnone\" style=\"min-height:340px\"><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-cover__background has-background-dim-50 has-background-dim\"></span><img class=\"wp-block-cover__image-background\" alt=\"\" src=\"{{media:mountains}}\" data-object-fit=\"cover\"/><div class=\"wp-block-cover__inner-container\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><a href=\"{{category:mountains}}\">Mountains</a></h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Ridges, passes and huts</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div></div><!-- /wp:cover --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:cover {\"url\":\"{{media:coast}}\",\"dimRatio\":50,\"minHeight\":340,\"minHeightUnit\":\"px\",\"align\":\"none\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-cover alignnone\" style=\"min-height:340px\"><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-cover__background has-background-dim-50 has-background-dim\"></span><img class=\"wp-block-cover__image-background\" alt=\"\" src=\"{{media:coast}}\" data-object-fit=\"cover\"/><div class=\"wp-block-cover__inner-container\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><a href=\"{{category:coastlines}}\">Coastlines</a></h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Cliffs, harbours and islands</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div></div><!-- /wp:cover --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns --><!-- wp:columns {\"align\":\"wide\",\"className\":\"wl-tiles\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide wl-tiles\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:cover {\"url\":\"{{media:city}}\",\"dimRatio\":50,\"minHeight\":340,\"minHeightUnit\":\"px\",\"align\":\"none\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-cover alignnone\" style=\"min-height:340px\"><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-cover__background has-background-dim-50 has-background-dim\"></span><img class=\"wp-block-cover__image-background\" alt=\"\" src=\"{{media:city}}\" data-object-fit=\"cover\"/><div class=\"wp-block-cover__inner-container\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><a href=\"{{category:city-breaks}}\">City Breaks</a></h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Slow weekends in old towns</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div></div><!-- /wp:cover --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:cover {\"url\":\"{{media:dunes}}\",\"dimRatio\":50,\"minHeight\":340,\"minHeightUnit\":\"px\",\"align\":\"none\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-cover alignnone\" style=\"min-height:340px\"><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-cover__background has-background-dim-50 has-background-dim\"></span><img class=\"wp-block-cover__image-background\" alt=\"\" src=\"{{media:dunes}}\" data-object-fit=\"cover\"/><div class=\"wp-block-cover__inner-container\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><a href=\"{{category:deserts}}\">Deserts</a></h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Sand, silence and stars</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div></div><!-- /wp:cover --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:cover {\"url\":\"{{media:lakes}}\",\"dimRatio\":50,\"minHeight\":340,\"minHeightUnit\":\"px\",\"align\":\"none\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-cover alignnone\" style=\"min-height:340px\"><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-cover__background has-background-dim-50 has-background-dim\"></span><img class=\"wp-block-cover__image-background\" alt=\"\" src=\"{{media:lakes}}\" data-object-fit=\"cover\"/><div class=\"wp-block-cover__inner-container\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><a href=\"{{category:forests-lakes}}\">Forests &amp; Lakes</a></h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Paddles and portages</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div></div><!-- /wp:cover --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">On the list for next year</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A winter crossing of the northern fjords by coastal ferry</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>The long desert railway, end to end</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A month on foot along the old salt road</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->",
   "template": "",
   "featured_media": "map"
  },
  {
   "slug": "about",
   "title": "About",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Wanderline is written by two people and one very patched backpack. We started it on a night train, somewhere between a border and a breakfast, as a way to remember the small things: the bakery at the start of a trail, the café that opens when the owner wakes up.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:image {\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\",\"align\":\"wide\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large alignwide\"><img src=\"{{media:road}}\" alt=\"An empty road winding toward distant mountains\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How we travel</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:columns --><div class=\"wp-block-columns\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Slowly</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We would rather spend a week in one valley than a day in seven cities.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Lightly</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>One bag each, public transport where we can, and our own two feet where we cannot.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Honestly</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Nobody pays for our opinions. When a place disappoints us, we say so, kindly.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns --><!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The best journeys answer questions that at the beginning you did not even think to ask.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If a story here sends you somewhere, we would love to hear about it.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:buttons --><div class=\"wp-block-buttons\"><!-- wp:button --><div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"{{page:contact}}\">Say hello</a></div><!-- /wp:button --></div><!-- /wp:buttons -->",
   "template": "",
   "featured_media": "road"
  },
  {
   "slug": "contact",
   "title": "Contact",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We read every message, usually from a train, and answer within a week or two, depending on the signal.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:columns --><div class=\"wp-block-columns\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Write to us</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>hello@wanderline.example<br>We love route questions, corrections and photos of your own trips.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Work together</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We occasionally write for travel magazines and tourism boards that share our slow-travel values.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns --><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Before you ask</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>We do not accept free trips in exchange for reviews.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Yes, you may use our route notes for your own walks.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>The backpack is twelve years old and not for sale.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->",
   "template": "",
   "featured_media": "map"
  }
 ],
 "posts": [
  {
   "slug": "twelve-days-on-the-high-ridge-trail",
   "title": "Twelve Days on the High Ridge Trail",
   "date": "2026-09-18T08:30:00",
   "excerpt": "Eleven passes, four huts, one storm and the best breakfast I have ever eaten, cooked on a stove the size of a teacup.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The trail begins behind a bakery. That is the first thing nobody tells you: the High Ridge, which climbs over eleven passes and never drops below the tree line once it gets going, starts with the smell of warm bread and a hand-painted arrow pointing between two houses.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>I had planned this walk for three winters. I had read every hut report, weighed every sock, and still, on the first morning, I stood at the arrow for a full minute before stepping onto the path, as though the mountains might notice me and say no.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The first four days: learning to go slowly</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Nobody walks fast on the High Ridge, and the ones who try are passed on day three by the ones who did not. The rhythm that works is boring and beautiful: up at first light, walking by seven, lunch on a warm rock, at the hut by three, asleep by nine.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:image {\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:ridge}}\" alt=\"A narrow ridge path running towards a range of jagged peaks\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Day four, between the Kelder and Ost passes.</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image --><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Route at a glance</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Day 1–2:</strong> village to the Kelder hut through larch forest.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Day 3–5:</strong> the long balcony path above the Siven valley.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Day 6:</strong> storm day, spent playing cards in the Ost hut.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Day 7–10:</strong> the high section, four passes above 2,600 metres.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Day 11–12:</strong> down through meadows to the lake and the train.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You do not conquer a ridge. You are allowed across it, one patient step at a time.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Note in the Ost hut logbook</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The storm on day six was the turning point. Twenty of us crammed into a stone hut built for twelve, wet boots steaming by the stove, while thunder rolled across the valley like furniture being moved upstairs. By morning the sky was scrubbed clean, and the whole range stood up in the first light as if it had been freshly painted.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:table {\"className\":\"wl-facts\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-table wl-facts\"><table><thead><tr><th>Practical</th><th>What worked</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Season</td><td>Late August to mid September</td></tr><tr><td>Huts</td><td>Book four weeks ahead; cash only above the tree line</td></tr><tr><td>Water</td><td>Springs every 2–3 hours; carry a filter anyway</td></tr><tr><td>Pack weight</td><td>Under 9 kg without food</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>I came down on day twelve with burnt ears, a lighter pack and a notebook full of people I will probably never see again. The bakery was still there at the bottom. I bought the same bread, and it tasted completely different.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "mountains"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "hiking",
    "slow-travel",
    "camping"
   ],
   "featured_media": "mountains",
