  {
   "slug": "chasing-the-last-light-along-the-salt-coast",
   "title": "Chasing the Last Light Along the Salt Coast",
   "date": "2026-09-04T19:10:00",
   "excerpt": "Six hundred kilometres of cliffs, fishing towns and sea-salt pans, driven backwards so that every evening ended facing the sunset.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Most people drive the Salt Coast north to south. We drove it the other way for one reason: every evening, the road turned west, and the sun went down directly in front of the windscreen like a reward for the day.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Towns worth the detour</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:columns --><div class=\"wp-block-columns\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Porta Lume</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A harbour of blue boats and a single café that opens when the owner wakes up. Worth waiting for.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Saline</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>White salt pans that turn pink at dusk. Walk the raised paths between them barefoot.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Capo Verro</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The lighthouse at the end of the world, or at least at the end of the road.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns --><!-- wp:image {\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:coast-cliffs}}\" alt=\"Golden cliffs dropping into a calm sea at sunset\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The cliffs below Capo Verro, twenty minutes before sunset.</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The trick to the coast is not to plan your days but to plan your evenings. Find the west-facing beach, the headland, the harbour wall. Arrive an hour early with bread, cheese and a cheap bottle of something local. Then just watch.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We stopped counting sunsets on the fourth night. We just started naming them.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>By the end of the week we had named eleven sunsets, including one that was mostly fog and one that we watched from a petrol station. Both counted.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "coastlines"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "road-trip",
    "sunsets",
    "photography"
   ],
   "featured_media": "coast",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false,
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Ilse",
     "email": "ilse@example.com",
     "content": "Driving it backwards for the sunsets is genius. Stealing this idea.",
     "date": "2026-09-06T08:02:00"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "a-slow-weekend-in-a-city-of-bridges",
   "title": "A Slow Weekend in a City of Bridges",
   "date": "2026-08-22T11:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Forty-one bridges, three river cafés and an argument about which one is the most beautiful. A weekend with no itinerary at all.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is a rule in our family for city weekends: no museums before lunch and no plans after dinner. The City of Bridges, built across a slow green river and its seven canals, is perfect for this kind of travel.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Saturday: crossing everything</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We set ourselves one silly goal: cross as many bridges as possible before noon. We managed twenty-three, including a rope bridge in a children's park and one that turned out to be somebody's private garden path. We apologised in two languages.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:image {\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:city-night}}\" alt=\"A city skyline at dusk reflected in a calm river\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The old quarter from the Iron Bridge at blue hour.</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image --><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Where we ate</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>The Lock Keeper's Kitchen</strong>: soup in bread bowls, eaten on the canal steps.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Café Seven</strong>: the best cardamom buns on the river, sold out by ten.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>The night market</strong> under the railway arches, every Saturday from six.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>On Sunday we did nothing at all, which in a city this beautiful is a full-time activity.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "city-breaks"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "food",
    "slow-travel",
    "architecture"
   ],
   "featured_media": "city",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false,
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Pieter",
     "email": "pieter@example.com",
     "content": "The rope bridge counts. I will hear no arguments.",
     "date": "2026-08-23T09:15:00"
    }
   ]
