  {
   "slug": "night-sky-over-the-red-dunes",
   "title": "Night Sky Over the Red Dunes",
   "date": "2026-08-08T21:45:00",
   "excerpt": "Three nights camping in the Ambra dunes, where the sand is warm until midnight and the Milky Way is bright enough to cast a shadow.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The desert is loud during the day. Wind on the dunes sounds like distant traffic, and the heat has its own hum. But at night, everything stops, and the sky starts.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:image {\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:dunes}}\" alt=\"Red sand dunes under a starry night sky\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Camp two, about 40 km from the nearest road.</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How to sleep under the stars (and actually sleep)</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Dig a shallow scoop for your hips before you lay out your mat.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Bring a warm layer: it can drop to 8°C before dawn.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Keep your boots inside your sleeping bag liner, and shake them out in the morning.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Turn off every light for twenty minutes and let your eyes adjust.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Out here, the dark is not empty. It is full of things you have never been able to see.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Our guide, on the first night</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>On the last night a shooting star crossed the whole sky, slowly enough that all six of us saw it and had time to point. Nobody said anything for a long while afterwards.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "deserts"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "camping",
    "stargazing",
    "photography"
   ],
   "featured_media": "dunes",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false,
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "paddling-the-seven-lakes-loop",
   "title": "Paddling the Seven Lakes Loop",
   "date": "2026-07-25T07:30:00",
   "excerpt": "Five days, seven lakes, six portages and a canoe that we carried more than we paddled. A loop through the quiet north.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Seven Lakes Loop is less a route than a necklace: seven long, dark lakes threaded together by short forest trails where you carry your canoe on your shoulders and your food in a barrel on your back.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:media-text {\"mediaType\":\"image\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-media-text is-stacked-on-mobile\"><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img src=\"{{media:lakes-mist}}\" alt=\"Morning mist lifting off a forest lake\" class=\"size-full\"/></figure><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Day one</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Calm water, a loon calling somewhere in the mist, and the first portage, which we took three trips to cross because we packed like amateurs.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div></div><!-- /wp:media-text --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What the portages taught us</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>By day three we had learned to pack in a single trip: one canoe, two barrels, two people. Everything else went into the fire or the pack-out bag. Packing lighter is not a skill, it is a decision you make again every morning.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:gallery {\"columns\":3,\"linkTo\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-gallery has-nested-images columns-3 is-cropped\"><!-- wp:image {\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:lakes}}\" alt=\"A canoe pulled up on a rocky shore\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image --><!-- wp:image {\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:lakes-mist}}\" alt=\"Mist over the lake at dawn\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image --><!-- wp:image {\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:ridge}}\" alt=\"A ridge above the forest\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image --></figure><!-- /wp:gallery --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We saw a total of nine other people in five days, and one moose, who was not impressed with us at all.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "forests-lakes"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "canoeing",
    "camping",
    "slow-travel"
   ],
   "featured_media": "lakes",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false,
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "what-i-pack-for-a-month-on-the-road",
   "title": "What I Pack for a Month on the Road",
   "date": "2026-07-10T09:00:00",
   "excerpt": "One 32-litre bag, every trip, for four years. Here is what goes in it, what I have stopped bringing, and the one thing I never leave behind.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>I have travelled with the same small bag for four years. It fits under an aeroplane seat, in the overhead rack of a night train, and on my lap in the back of a minibus with a goat. That last one is not hypothetical.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The list</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:columns --><div class=\"wp-block-columns\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Clothes</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>3 merino t-shirts</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>1 long-sleeve shirt</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>2 pairs of trousers (one that becomes shorts)</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>1 warm fleece</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>1 light rain shell</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>4 pairs of socks</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Everything else</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A small first-aid kit</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A head torch</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A paper notebook and two pens</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A universal plug and one cable</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A scarf that is also a towel, blanket and pillow</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What I stopped bringing</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A second pair of shoes, a laptop, a guidebook for every country, and the idea that I needed to be ready for everything. You can buy almost anything almost anywhere. What you cannot buy is a lighter shoulder.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Pack for the trip you are taking, not the one you are afraid of.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "categories": [
    "field-notes"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "gear",
    "packing"
   ],
   "featured_media": "pack",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false,
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Noor",
     "email": "noor@example.com",
     "content": "The scarf-towel-blanket is the real hero of this list.",
     "date": "2026-07-11T12:30:00"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "island-hopping-without-a-plan",
   "title": "Island Hopping Without a Plan",
   "date": "2026-06-27T10:20:00",
   "excerpt": "Ten days, five islands and not a single ferry booked in advance. What happens when you let the timetable decide.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The rule was simple: every morning, walk to the harbour and take whichever boat left first. No bookings, no plans, no idea where we would sleep.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:image {\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:islands}}\" alt=\"Small green islands scattered across a turquoise sea\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The islands, in the order the ferries chose</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Vela</strong>: a fishing island with one road and four hundred cats.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Ostra</strong>: whitewashed and windy, with a monastery on every hill.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Little Mira</strong>: no cars, no shops, one tavern, perfect.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Kaldi</strong>: the busy one, where we ate too much and slept too little.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Vela again</strong>, because the cats missed us.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not planning is its own kind of planning. You trade certainty for surprise, and on islands, surprise is almost always the better deal.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We slept in a fisherman's spare room, a monastery guest house and, for one memorable night, on the deck of a cargo ferry under a blanket of stars.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "coastlines"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "islands",
    "slow-travel",
    "ferries"
   ],
   "featured_media": "islands",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false,
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "eating-through-the-old-market-quarter",
   "title": "Eating My Way Through the Old Market Quarter",
   "date": "2026-06-12T13:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Eleven stalls, three grandmothers, one very large pastry and a list of everything I ate in a single afternoon.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Old Market Quarter is less a market than a maze: covered lanes, striped awnings, stalls stacked three high, and somewhere in the middle, a fountain where everyone sits to eat what they just bought.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:image {\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:market}}\" alt=\"Colourful striped awnings over a busy market lane\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The spice lane, around noon.</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The afternoon menu</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Flatbread straight from a clay oven, with herbs and oil</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A paper cone of roasted chickpeas</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Grilled corn with chilli and lime</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A honey pastry the size of my hand, recommended by a grandmother who would not take no for an answer</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Mint tea, three times, because it kept being offered</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The best advice I got all day came from a spice seller: walk the market twice, once to look and once to buy. I did not follow it, and I regret nothing.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>By sunset I had spent less than the price of a restaurant dinner, learned four new words for bread and been invited to a wedding. I could not go, which I still regret.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "city-breaks"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "food",
    "markets"
   ],
   "featured_media": "market",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false,
   "comments": []
  }
 ],
